jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.37~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * PowerFlex NVMe/TCP could not have connected at all - the default data
    path for this family. Two reasons, both settled by reading Dell's own
    ansible-powerflex module, which shows a real SDT object:
    - The host connected to storagePort. An SDT publishes nvmePort 4420,
      storagePort 12200 and discoveryPort 8009; storagePort carries
      SDS-to-SDT traffic and is not what a host connects to. Every
      'nvme connect' would have been refused.
    - The subsystem NQN was read from the SDT, which has no NQN field of any
      kind. nvme_connect croaks without one, so activate_storage would have
      died before connecting anything. It is discovered with 'nvme discover'
      against the discovery port now, once per storage, and cached.
  * A host no longer connects to an address whose role is StorageOnly.
  * docs/TESTING.md records the SDT field names, the three ports and the
    address roles, with what Dell's module shows for each.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 08:24:45 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.36~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * PowerFlex: creating a volume sends volumeSizeInKb and falls back to
    volumeSizeInGb. The ScaleIO REST reference documents the first, Dell's
    own python-powerflex SDK sends the second, and creating a volume is the
    very first thing anyone does with this plugin - so an array that accepts
    only the other spelling no longer stops the first 'pvesm alloc'. The
    fallback is taken only on a 4xx, which means the array created nothing;
    a 5xx may have taken effect and is never retried.
  * docs/TESTING.md: several PowerFlex field names are no longer marked
    unverified. Dell's own ansible-powerflex collection documents
    ancestorVolumeId, creationTime, protectionDomainId and the contents of
    mappedSdcInfo, and confirms the 8 GB allocation unit and that the array
    rounds up.

