jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.24~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * PowerStore: the ilike wildcard is now '*', which is what every example in
    the Dell PowerStore REST API Developers Guide spells it as. The plugin
    had been sending '%'. A wildcard the array reads as an ordinary character
    matches nothing, so a name-prefix listing would have come back empty and
    every volume would have disappeared from PVE while the array still held
    them - silently, and indistinguishably from an empty storage.
  * PowerStore: a name-prefix listing that comes back empty is now retried
    without the filter and matched locally, with one warning naming the
    cause. The wildcard question can no longer make the plugin lose volumes,
    whichever form this appliance accepts.
  * PowerStore: a name filter the array applies more broadly than a prefix
    can no longer pull another storage's volumes into this one's listing.
  * PowerStore: the DELL-EMC-TOKEN is refreshed from any response that
    carries one. Dell documents the CSRF token as something to fetch with a
    GET before each write, which leaves open whether the array reissues it
    during a session; if it does, holding the login-time token would fail
    every write while every read kept working.
  * PowerStore: clearing a session now empties the cookie jar, so a re-login
    after a 401 does not present the rejected auth_cookie alongside fresh
    credentials.
  * docs/TESTING.md records what has now been read from the PowerStore
    developers guide - the session and CSRF rules, the filter form, the
    operator list, the wildcard - so a first tester can tell it apart from
    what is still inferred.

